<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>{title}</title><meta name="description" content="Discover American Mah Jongg, Chinese regional Mahjong, Japanese riichi and the history of Mahjong in Korea."/><link rel="canonical" href="https://mymahjongera.com/world/"/><style>body{font:18px/1.75 Arial,sans-serif;margin:0;background:#fffaf7;color:#4d2540}main{max-width:800px;margin:60px auto;padding:0 24px}h1{font:bold clamp(38px,6vw,60px)/1.1 Georgia,serif}h2{font-family:Georgia,serif;margin-top:44px}a{color:#ad4270}nav{padding:22px;background:#fce8ee}</style></head><body><nav><a href="/">← My Mahjong Era</a></nav><main><p>AROUND THE WORLD</p><h1>One table. Many ways to play.</h1><p>Mahjong has inspired regional traditions, adaptations, friendships and communities. This is a starting map, not a claim that each country follows one uniform rulebook.</p><h2>United States · American Mah Jongg</h2><p>Often played with jokers, racks, a Charleston tile exchange and an annually issued card of winning hands. <a href="/learn/american-mahjong/">Begin here</a>.</p><h2>China · Many regional styles</h2><p>Chinese Mahjong includes numerous regional rulesets, including Cantonese and Sichuan styles. Scoring, permissible hands and special tiles differ significantly.</p><h2>Japan · Riichi Mahjong</h2><p>Japanese riichi features a distinct scoring system, yaku requirements, dora bonus tiles and strategic calls including declaring riichi.</p><h2>Korea · Regional history and variations</h2><p>Mahjong has a history in Korea, but it is less common in mainstream social play than some other tabletop games. Specific Korean rule descriptions need to be tied to a verified variation rather than presented as one universal national standard.</p><p><a href="/">Return home</a></p></main></body></html>